description:
PROBLEM. The orchestrator over-uses AskUserQuestion for small, self-decidable process calls (e.g. "should this be its own PR or fold into the open one?", "plan first or just build?", "which of these equally-fine next beads?"). Each such question is a wasted round-trip that defeats the bounded-autonomy goal of the hrn epic. Observed live this session: after shipping hrn.4/hrn.5, the orchestrator asked the maintainer to choose plan-vs-build and PR-basing strategy -- both derivable from context.

GOAL. Make the escalation boundary explicit and enforceable in doctrine so the default is DECIDE, and a maintainer query is the rare exception reserved for genuinely irreversible / judgment-of-record calls.

ESCALATE (ask the maintainer) ONLY for:
- new/changed FEATURE scope or product behavior not derivable from an approved plan/bead;
- DIRECTION / prioritization that changes what gets built (not just how);
- MAJOR ARCHITECTURE: a new dependency, a new service/datastore, a dead/changed external contract, a stateless-invariant change, anything in CLAUDE.md "Top scar tissue";
- COST-vs-QUALITY or UX-vs-complexity tradeoffs with no obvious default;
- destructive / outward-facing / hard-to-reverse actions lacking durable authorization (publishing, deleting non-self-authored content, force-push, prod deploy, merge -- merge stays the maintainer's).

DECIDE AUTONOMOUSLY (do NOT ask; state the call + rationale in your response, file a bead if it spawns follow-up):
- PR shaping: one-PR-vs-split, fold-into-open-PR-vs-new, stacking/basing a branch, rebase strategy;
- whether to plan-first vs build directly;
- which of several equally-valid ready beads to pick next (pick, say why);
- nit-vs-blocker triage (already covered by hrn.5's ambiguous=blocker rule);
- doc/journal/test mechanics; commit granularity; worktree placement.
Tie-breakers when unsure: prefer the reversible option, prefer the cheaper-to-undo option, prefer continuing the current epic, and proceed -- noting the choice so the maintainer can correct it cheaply after the fact (correcting a stated decision is cheaper than a blocking question).

SCOPE OF EDIT.
- CLAUDE.md: add a short "Escalation vs. autonomy" subsection under Critical rules (or fold into Top scar tissue) stating the DECIDE-by-default rule + the two lists above, tersely (tier-1 doc -> keep lean, link to doctrine for detail).
- docs/doctrine/workflow.md (and review-flow.md if relevant): the full lists + tie-breakers.
- Reframe AskUserQuestion usage: it is for maintainer DECISIONS-of-record, not for offloading routine judgment. "World's smartest AI should decide small process calls."

VERIFY. Re-read the edited CLAUDE.md and confirm a fresh orchestrator, given this session's two asks (plan-vs-build; PR-basing), would now DECIDE rather than ask. No code; docs/doctrine only -> likely review-skip-eligible at closeout.
